-- title:
--   The flat condition is a deformation condition
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be any commutative ring (no local, Noetherian or completeness hypothesis is imposed), let $p$ be a natural number (primality is not assumed) and let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers. The assertion is that the predicate [`GaloisRep.flatCondition 𝒪 p S`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L47) on two-dimensional adically continuous representations $\rho$ of $\operatorname{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ over local $\mathcal O$-algebras $A$ satisfies the project's axioms [`GaloisRep.IsDeformationCondition`](def/GaloisRep_DeformationCondition.html#L19). Here `flatCondition 𝒪 p S` $\rho$ is the conjunction of three clauses, none of which mentions $\mathcal O$: (i) $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and, for all $n$, all $\sigma$ and all $a$ such that $\sigma\mu=\mu^{a}$ on $p^{n}$-th roots of unity, $\det\rho(\sigma)-a\in(p^{n})A$; (ii) flatness at $p$ — the residue field of $A$ is finite and, for every ideal $I$ with $A/I$ finite, there is a commutative, cocommutative Hopf algebra $H$ over the subring of $\mathbb Q$ of rationals with denominator coprime to $p$, finite and flat as a module over that subring, together with a bijection from the convolution group of algebra maps $H\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ onto $\rho.V/I\rho.V$ which is additive and intertwines the Galois action on points with the induced action on $\rho.V/I\rho.V$; (iii) inertia acts trivially at every valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over a prime $q\notin S$. The five axioms verified are: invariance under equivalence of representations over Artinian test $\mathcal O$-algebras (local structure map, residue field generated by $\mathcal O$, Artinian); stability under base change along local $\mathcal O$-algebra maps of test algebras; descent along injective such maps; descent along a jointly injective pair $P\to A$, $P\to B$ surjecting onto the fibre product over $C$; and, for Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $A$ complete for the maximal-ideal topology with local structure map and residue field generated by $\mathcal O$, the equivalence of the condition for $\rho$ with its validity after every surjective local $\mathcal O$-algebra map to a test algebra.
--
--   Classically this is the statement that the flat deformation problem satisfies Mazur's conditions on a deformation condition, so that the associated functor is relatively representable; it goes back to Ramakrishna's variant of Mazur's deformation functor and to Conrad's treatment of the flat deformation functor. The formal statement differs from the textbook one in that $\mathcal O$ is an arbitrary commutative ring and $p$ an arbitrary natural number, and in that flatness at $p$ is expressed as the existence, at each finite level, of a finite flat commutative and cocommutative Hopf algebra over the localisation of $\mathbb Z$ away from $p$ whose $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points realise that level Galois-equivariantly. It is the input `h𝒟` to the representability of the flat deformation problem, used to construct the flat deformation ring attached to a residual representation, both in the abstract form and for the mod $p$ representation of a semistable integral Weierstrass model.
